{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Utterance of the man.</b> He called himself 'man' (gever) because he likened himself to a rooster that knows how to determine the hours, and there is none in the world who can determine the hour of the anger of the Holy One, blessed be He, except Balaam among human beings and the rooster among birds — and both are equal in this wisdom. And they said that the rooster cries seven times at night, and likewise in Balaam we find that he lifted up his proverb seven times. And the rooster is an adulterer more than all birds, and therefore roosters were not fit for the altar. And likewise Balaam was an adulterer, as the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted from ״הסכן הסכנתי״ — which is from the language of (I Kings 1:4) ״ותהי למלך סוכנת״; and so too his counsel was to cause Israel to stumble into harlotry with the daughters of Moab." "<b>Whose eye is opened (שתום העין).</b> Open of eye, and it is the opposite of סתום (closed); and in the language of the Sages, of blessed memory: ״כדי שישתום ויסתום״ — its meaning being, so that he may open and close. And know that all apprehensions are three: they are sight, hearing, and knowledge — and all three of them you will find in Balaam: sight, in that he said שתום העין; hearing, שומע אמרי אל; knowledge, ויודע דעת עליון. And we find all three of them in a single verse concerning the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Exodus 3:7): ״ראה ראיתי את עני עמי״, etc. And since sight and hearing apply to people and do not befit the Holy One, blessed be He, the verse is to be interpreted: how did I see and how did I hear — for I knew his affliction, for knowledge includes both of them."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:44.646247202Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}